From: lagz0ne <15101444+lagz0ne@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 10:37:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 34/40] perf(canvas): scale the stage grids while the zoom
 moves, paint them at rest

The dot grid is one composited layer the size of the view, a repeating
background whose period follows the zoom. Every zoom step wrote a new
background-size, which repaints the whole layer: a 60% to 95% pinch over a
page rastered it on each of its 30 frames (140 tiles, and 139 back down).
The pixel grid from 300% did the same (120 tiles in a deep pinch).

Map and canvas apps scale what they already rastered while the zoom moves
and draw it afresh once it rests. The grids now do that: while the camera
zooms each keeps the period it was painted at and its transform carries
the ratio since (the translate is the one for the camera's period, so dots
stay on the camera's lattice). It is painted again when that ratio leaves
its band, and exactly once the camera rests, on the same 120 ms rest the
chrome's --zoom already waits for. A pan is still one transform write.

The band is one-sided: a grid is only magnified, from 1 to 1.1, so the
layer (the stage plus one period) still covers the stage. Zooming out, it
is painted for one band below the camera. Rejected:
- a two-sided band with a layer large enough to be scaled down: wider than
  one raster tile, it tripled the tiles of every repaint (15 against 4.7),
  so a pinch cost 45-120 tiles whatever the band;
- a 1.2 band (15 tiles up, 49 down): screenshots of every step of a pinch
  against the exact grid show the dots swell, 1.4 to 2.1 times their ink,
  most while zooming out. At 1.1 they stay within 0.7-1.0 of it, the
  spread the exact grid itself shows from step to step as its faint dots
  are resampled; at 1.05 the same, for more repaints.

Numbers (trace-pinch.mjs + raster.mjs, swiftshader GPU raster, 188-frame
page, two rounds each, counts identical in both; load 10-19):
- B1 pinch 60% to 95%: dot grid 140 tiles in 30 frames -> 24 in 5; editor
  raster tasks 205 -> 89.
- B2 pinch back: 139 in 30 -> 79 in 6 (a zoom-out repaint is rastered at
  the layer's scale, 15 tiles, against 4-5 at rest); raster tasks 226 -> 166.
- Deep pinch 320% to 500% and back: dot grid 450 -> 75 / 93 tiles, pixel
  grid 120 -> 20 / 24, editor raster tasks 600 -> 125 / 147.
- Pans: no grid raster, as before. Frames' renderer: unchanged.
- At rest the grid is pixel-identical to before at fit, 60%, 95% and 400%,
  and so is the whole stage, but for the frame previews at fit, which
  differ by up to 5 levels between two runs of the old build too.

